        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "western North American boreal forests",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "It was reported that the growth of western North American boreal forests is constrained by both the increasing costs of autotrophic respiration41,42 and frequent summer drought9,12,43,44, because higher temperature and longer growing season will accelerate greater ecosystem respiration and may exacerbate water stress in summer.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Drought",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "However, drought events associated with high temperature and low water availability can decrease plant photosynthetic uptake6-8. In regions exposed to summer drought, an increase of leaf area in earlier spring can accelerate soil drying, and lead to increased vulnerability of GPP in summer9,10. In terms of net carbon balance, carbon loss during summer drought can negate increased uptake in warmer springs and autumns11-13, related to the negative covariance between increased spring productivity and decreased yearly productivity.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
